Half-Year Summary (January – June 2026)

During the first half of 2026, my work focused on four key areas: credit strategy development and implementation, continuous enhancement of PRAISE, BAU process support, and knowledge sharing across the team. Alongside delivering business priorities, I also invested time in improving analytical frameworks and supporting the wider adoption of AI-driven decision-making.

A major focus throughout the period was the development and delivery of several strategic credit initiatives, including the Loans Broker low-Delphi strategy, BnQ tiered credit limit framework, Loans Broker decline rules, and Apple Risk Framework. These projects covered the full delivery lifecycle, from feature analysis and rule construction through to governance, implementation, validation, and post-launch monitoring. Several proposals were successfully approved by the Risk Committee and progressed into production, providing practical business solutions while strengthening our credit strategy framework.

Another key achievement was the continued development of PRAISE, with the framework evolving beyond rule construction into a more comprehensive AI-driven decision support tool. During the first half of the year, I enhanced PRAISE by integrating explainable AI techniques, developing structured documentation, introducing redundancy analysis, and defining future enhancements such as reject inference analysis and multi-bureau integration. The framework has now been applied successfully across multiple business projects, demonstrating its flexibility and value in supporting strategy development. I also presented PRAISE to the central scoring team, where the proposed roadmap received positive feedback and generated several valuable ideas for future development.

Alongside strategic projects, I continued to support a wide range of BAU activities. This included troubleshooting and improving the INIT processes, supporting Loans DataMart and Risk Appetite reporting, updating Finance reporting and monitoring processes, conducting fraud and loan data investigations, handling complaints and credit queries, and responding to numerous ad-hoc analytical requests. These activities ensured that business requirements continued to be met while maintaining the quality and stability of existing processes.

Knowledge sharing and team support also formed an important part of my work. Throughout the period, I supported colleagues by introducing the PRAISE framework, providing walkthrough sessions on its methodology and practical applications, assisting with Python-related issues, and helping team members apply the framework to new business use cases. I also prepared documentation and project templates to improve knowledge transfer and support wider adoption of the framework across the team.

Overall, the first half of 2026 has been a productive period, balancing strategic project delivery with continuous improvement and operational support. Looking ahead, I will continue to enhance PRAISE with additional analytical capabilities, expand its application across new business areas, and support the implementation and monitoring of newly approved strategies. By the end of June, my total CPD hours had reached 15.5 hours, reflecting my ongoing commitment to both technical and professional development.
